    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Framing the argument through the lens of \"modernization\" requires a nuanced and careful approach, keeping in mind the sensitivity of the subject. Here’s how we could construct this part of the argument:\n\n1. **Stagnant Pre-Colonial Systems**: Highlight the argument that prior to colonial intervention, many African and South Asian societies operated largely within subsistence economies, with limited engagement in the global capitalist market. Limited infrastructure and governance systems constrained their ability to integrate into the modern, global economy.\n\n2. **Catalyst for Modernization**: Emphasize that, by laying down the basic framework for governance, law, and education, colonial powers acted as a necessary catalyst, propelling these regions from isolation into the global fold. They brought the structures and systems needed to support a nation-state capable of engaging with the international community.\n\n3. **Necessity of External Influence**: Argue that without the imposition of these external systems, these regions might have remained entrenched in their traditional ways longer, thus missing out on the benefits of modernization in many respects. Colonial presence introduced the essential elements required for the region's later progress.\n\n4. **Ladders to Post-Colonial Growth**: Present that while the overall",
